WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.496
Earlier, we saw a firewall and we used it to block a port from communication.

00:06.496 --> 00:14.454
You may recall that on most firewalls, inbound connections tend to be blocked and outbound connections tend to be allowed.

00:14.454 --> 00:26.421
You may need to open an inbound port to let an app through, like in this example, port 1433 for SQL Server, a database application server.

00:26.421 --> 00:39.315
I have Windows Firewall with Advanced Security open, I will click Inbound Rules, I'll click New Rule, and then I'll click Port.

00:39.315 --> 00:56.049
I'll click Next, make sure this is set to TCP, indicate that I want to open port 1433, click Next, make sure this is set to Allow the connection, click Next again,

00:56.049 --> 01:12.157
I'll apply it to all aspects of this firewall, I'll click Next one more time, I'll name this Allow SQL Server port 1433, and I'll click Finish.

01:12.157 --> 01:19.971
And now this device will have no trouble accepting incoming connections from a SQL server.

